1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:01,350
You wake up at midnight,

2
00:00:01,710 --> 00:00:02,450
look in the mirror,

3
00:00:02,670 --> 00:00:04,470
and the voices in your head are gone.

4
00:00:04,650 --> 00:00:06,810
Instead, ticking silently on your

5
00:00:06,810 --> 00:00:08,730
forehead is a luminous digital clock.

6
00:00:08,730 --> 00:00:10,290
Fifty-two years.

7
00:00:10,290 --> 00:00:12,350
One hundred and eighty-four days.

8
00:00:12,350 --> 00:00:13,510
Fourteen hours.

9
00:00:13,770 --> 00:00:15,530
And counting down with every heartbeat.

10
00:00:16,030 --> 00:00:17,090
You touch your brow.

11
00:00:17,530 --> 00:00:18,130
Cold skin.

12
00:00:18,410 --> 00:00:19,470
But the digits remain.

13
00:00:19,970 --> 00:00:21,450
You step out onto your balcony.

14
00:00:21,850 --> 00:00:24,150
A street cleaner is sweeping autumn leaves.

15
00:00:24,150 --> 00:00:25,150
Above his head:

16
00:00:25,430 --> 00:00:27,070
four years, two months.

17
00:00:27,390 --> 00:00:28,990
A teenager laughs on a skateboard:

18
00:00:28,990 --> 00:00:30,810
sixty-eight years.

19
00:00:31,010 --> 00:00:32,310
And then you see a young,

20
00:00:32,310 --> 00:00:34,770
athletic jogger in neon running shoes.

21
00:00:34,990 --> 00:00:36,850
His clock is glowing dark red:

22
00:00:36,990 --> 00:00:37,910
Zero years.

23
00:00:38,250 --> 00:00:39,070
Zero days.

24
00:00:39,450 --> 00:00:40,990
One hour and fourteen minutes.

25
00:00:41,330 --> 00:00:42,830
For the next seven days,

26
00:00:42,990 --> 00:00:44,870
you can see the exact expiration

27
00:00:44,870 --> 00:00:46,930
date of every human soul on earth.

28
00:00:47,090 --> 00:00:48,270
And by day seven,

29
00:00:48,390 --> 00:00:50,830
you will realize that humans don't fear dying.

30
00:00:50,830 --> 00:00:53,490
We fear realizing how much of life we threw

31
00:00:53,490 --> 00:00:55,370
away while pretending we were immortal.

32
00:00:55,710 --> 00:00:58,370
At first, your instinct is sheer panic.

33
00:00:58,550 --> 00:01:01,250
Because you realize that every room you enter is filled

34
00:01:01,250 --> 00:01:04,090
with people walking toward invisible trapdoors.

35
00:01:04,090 --> 00:01:05,430
At the subway station,

36
00:01:05,630 --> 00:01:07,470
hundreds of numbers bob in unison.

37
00:01:07,470 --> 00:01:08,730
Thirty years.

38
00:01:08,730 --> 00:01:09,970
Fifty years.

39
00:01:09,970 --> 00:01:11,190
Twelve years.

40
00:01:11,430 --> 00:01:12,850
And you notice something bizarre:

41
00:01:13,230 --> 00:01:15,530
The people with fifty or sixty years on their

42
00:01:15,530 --> 00:01:17,210
clocks are the ones looking miserable.

43
00:01:17,210 --> 00:01:19,370
They sit with slouched shoulders,

44
00:01:19,370 --> 00:01:21,390
doom-scrolling short videos,

45
00:01:21,770 --> 00:01:23,930
staring into screens with vacant eyes.

46
00:01:24,170 --> 00:01:25,490
They take out their phones,

47
00:01:25,930 --> 00:01:26,710
ignore their children,

48
00:01:27,070 --> 00:01:28,650
and snap at the ticket vendor.

49
00:01:29,010 --> 00:01:29,290
Why?

50
00:01:29,590 --> 00:01:30,970
Because when your clock says

51
00:01:30,970 --> 00:01:32,550
you have fifty years left,

52
00:01:32,790 --> 00:01:35,290
each individual hour feels essentially worthless.

53
00:01:35,650 --> 00:01:37,870
You treat time like Monopoly money because

54
00:01:37,870 --> 00:01:39,590
you assume the bank never closes.

55
00:01:40,190 --> 00:01:42,110
Roman philosopher Seneca wrote about

56
00:01:42,110 --> 00:01:43,830
this two thousand years ago:

57
00:01:43,830 --> 00:01:46,190
You live as if you are destined to live forever.

58
00:01:46,510 --> 00:01:48,990
No thought of your frailty ever enters your head.

59
00:01:49,010 --> 00:01:50,330
When death feels distant,

60
00:01:50,810 --> 00:01:51,750
life feels cheap.

61
00:01:52,348 --> 00:01:53,168
On day two,

62
00:01:53,408 --> 00:01:55,388
you can't stop thinking about the jogger.

63
00:01:55,748 --> 00:01:56,488
The neon shoes.

64
00:01:56,888 --> 00:01:58,628
The one hour and fourteen minutes.

65
00:01:59,008 --> 00:02:00,328
You check local news.

66
00:02:00,328 --> 00:02:02,628
At one fifteen yesterday morning,

67
00:02:02,768 --> 00:02:05,308
a delivery truck ran a light three blocks away.

68
00:02:05,588 --> 00:02:06,728
A jogger was struck.

69
00:02:06,968 --> 00:02:08,368
The clock wasn't a probability.

70
00:02:08,908 --> 00:02:09,848
It wasn't a warning.

71
00:02:10,208 --> 00:02:11,168
It was a fact.

72
00:02:11,428 --> 00:02:12,288
By day three,

73
00:02:12,528 --> 00:02:14,588
the knowledge becomes an unbearable weight.

74
00:02:14,928 --> 00:02:16,228
You walk into your office.

75
00:02:16,608 --> 00:02:18,748
Kevin's clock says forty-one years.

76
00:02:19,048 --> 00:02:21,528
Your manager's clock says twenty-eight years.

77
00:02:21,528 --> 00:02:23,488
Then an elderly courier delivers a

78
00:02:23,488 --> 00:02:25,068
package to the reception desk.

79
00:02:25,408 --> 00:02:26,908
His timer: two days,

80
00:02:27,168 --> 00:02:27,868
three hours.

81
00:02:28,228 --> 00:02:28,908
He is laughing,

82
00:02:29,408 --> 00:02:31,288
telling a joke to the receptionist,

83
00:02:31,408 --> 00:02:34,248
entirely unaware that forty-eight hours from now,

84
00:02:34,468 --> 00:02:36,008
his story on earth is finished.

85
00:02:36,328 --> 00:02:36,728
You freeze.

86
00:02:37,288 --> 00:02:38,308
Do you tell him?

87
00:02:38,528 --> 00:02:39,948
Excuse me, sir.

88
00:02:40,268 --> 00:02:40,968
Go home.

89
00:02:41,268 --> 00:02:42,128
Call your daughter.

90
00:02:42,468 --> 00:02:43,468
Cancel your shifts.

91
00:02:43,808 --> 00:02:44,748
What would he say?

92
00:02:45,128 --> 00:02:46,168
He'd think you're insane.

93
00:02:46,668 --> 00:02:48,228
He'd call building security.

94
00:02:48,768 --> 00:02:51,308
Psychologists call this Terror Management Theory:

95
00:02:51,308 --> 00:02:54,048
humans maintain elaborate cultural systems

96
00:02:54,048 --> 00:02:56,488
and daily routines specifically to suppress

97
00:02:56,488 --> 00:02:58,748
the terrifying awareness of our mortality.

98
00:02:59,128 --> 00:03:01,268
If you tear away that veil without warning,

99
00:03:01,628 --> 00:03:02,988
you don't liberate someone.

100
00:03:03,428 --> 00:03:05,448
You induce psychological collapse.

101
00:03:05,728 --> 00:03:07,688
You realize the horror of omniscience:

102
00:03:08,068 --> 00:03:10,668
knowing the future doesn't give you control over it.

103
00:03:10,808 --> 00:03:13,488
It only forces you to grieve before the loss happens.

104
00:03:14,267 --> 00:03:15,127
On day four,

105
00:03:15,347 --> 00:03:16,587
you refuse to be passive.

106
00:03:16,967 --> 00:03:18,967
You decide you're going to break the rule.

107
00:03:18,967 --> 00:03:20,307
At the city library,

108
00:03:20,667 --> 00:03:23,667
a college student is studying alone at a wooden desk.

109
00:03:24,007 --> 00:03:25,727
Her timer: thirty-five minutes.

110
00:03:26,247 --> 00:03:27,787
You sit two tables away,

111
00:03:28,207 --> 00:03:29,307
watching the seconds melt.

112
00:03:29,647 --> 00:03:30,907
At ten minutes left,

113
00:03:31,187 --> 00:03:33,547
she packs her laptop into her canvas bag,

114
00:03:33,867 --> 00:03:35,907
stands up, and walks toward the exit.

115
00:03:36,267 --> 00:03:37,267
You follow her.

116
00:03:37,407 --> 00:03:39,167
She steps onto the crosswalk.

117
00:03:39,447 --> 00:03:41,627
A city bus turns the corner fast,

118
00:03:41,907 --> 00:03:43,207
blinding rain on the windshield.

119
00:03:43,207 --> 00:03:44,487
You don't think.

120
00:03:44,947 --> 00:03:45,907
You sprint forward,

121
00:03:46,187 --> 00:03:47,067
grab her backpack,

122
00:03:47,507 --> 00:03:49,427
and yank her backward onto the sidewalk.

123
00:03:49,427 --> 00:03:51,267
The bus roars past,

124
00:03:51,267 --> 00:03:53,627
spraying puddle water across her jeans.

125
00:03:54,087 --> 00:03:55,787
She stumbles, gasping,

126
00:03:56,147 --> 00:03:57,287
furious and terrified:

127
00:03:57,767 --> 00:03:58,927
What is wrong with you?!

128
00:03:59,187 --> 00:04:00,087
Let go of me!

129
00:04:00,327 --> 00:04:01,887
You look at her forehead.

130
00:04:01,887 --> 00:04:04,087
The timer didn't reset to eighty years.

131
00:04:04,307 --> 00:04:05,307
It didn't disappear.

132
00:04:05,747 --> 00:04:08,247
The digits simply shifted by sixteen minutes:

133
00:04:08,607 --> 00:04:09,547
Zero hours.

134
00:04:09,547 --> 00:04:10,747
Sixteen minutes.

135
00:04:10,747 --> 00:04:12,507
Twenty-eight seconds.

136
00:04:12,507 --> 00:04:14,707
Your stomach drops into ice water.

137
00:04:14,967 --> 00:04:16,227
You didn't cure mortality.

138
00:04:16,727 --> 00:04:18,187
You merely postponed the collision.

139
00:04:18,707 --> 00:04:21,607
Death wasn't a specific bus on a specific street.

140
00:04:21,887 --> 00:04:24,227
It was an inevitable biological and physical

141
00:04:24,227 --> 00:04:26,807
certainty woven into the fabric of time.

142
00:04:27,087 --> 00:04:28,007
By day five,

143
00:04:28,127 --> 00:04:30,287
after shadowing her and watching her collapse

144
00:04:30,287 --> 00:04:32,747
from an undiagnosed aneurysm in a pharmacy,

145
00:04:33,167 --> 00:04:35,607
you understand: You cannot cheat the ledger.

146
00:04:35,867 --> 00:04:38,567
You can only torture yourself trying to outrun it.

147
00:04:39,093 --> 00:04:40,193
On day six,

148
00:04:40,193 --> 00:04:41,733
you stop looking at strangers.

149
00:04:42,233 --> 00:04:43,613
You take the train home.

150
00:04:43,893 --> 00:04:46,473
You open the front door of your childhood house.

151
00:04:46,753 --> 00:04:48,733
The smell of cinnamon and roasted tea.

152
00:04:49,053 --> 00:04:51,393
Your mother turns from the stove with a warm smile.

153
00:04:51,853 --> 00:04:54,253
Your father looks up from his crossword puzzle.

154
00:04:54,633 --> 00:04:56,593
And before you can stop yourself,

155
00:04:56,953 --> 00:04:58,153
your eyes flick upward.

156
00:04:58,393 --> 00:04:59,913
Mother: seven years,

157
00:05:00,253 --> 00:05:00,873
four months.

158
00:05:01,313 --> 00:05:02,813
Father: three years,

159
00:05:03,033 --> 00:05:03,773
eight months.

160
00:05:04,133 --> 00:05:06,073
A physical blow strikes your chest.

161
00:05:06,193 --> 00:05:07,993
You've always known intellectually that

162
00:05:07,993 --> 00:05:09,713
your parents wouldn't live forever.

163
00:05:10,013 --> 00:05:11,413
Every human knows this.

164
00:05:11,713 --> 00:05:13,013
Yet we postpone visits.

165
00:05:13,493 --> 00:05:15,433
We answer their phone calls with rushed,

166
00:05:15,773 --> 00:05:16,693
distracted voices.

167
00:05:17,253 --> 00:05:18,253
We tell ourselves:

168
00:05:18,733 --> 00:05:20,133
I'll visit next weekend.

169
00:05:20,533 --> 00:05:22,453
I'll take them to dinner next month.

170
00:05:22,633 --> 00:05:24,213
When work calms down.

171
00:05:24,213 --> 00:05:25,673
Standing in that kitchen,

172
00:05:26,033 --> 00:05:27,873
looking at three years and eight months,

173
00:05:28,113 --> 00:05:31,153
the arithmetic of your entire life rewrites itself.

174
00:05:31,393 --> 00:05:32,913
If you visit twice a year,

175
00:05:33,213 --> 00:05:34,593
three years means you will see

176
00:05:34,593 --> 00:05:36,373
your father six more times.

177
00:05:36,373 --> 00:05:37,493
Not hundreds.

178
00:05:37,813 --> 00:05:38,373
Six.

179
00:05:38,373 --> 00:05:39,913
For the rest of the evening,

180
00:05:40,233 --> 00:05:41,953
you don't check your phone once.

181
00:05:42,253 --> 00:05:43,753
You ask your father about his

182
00:05:43,753 --> 00:05:45,093
childhood in the countryside.

183
00:05:45,613 --> 00:05:47,273
You listen to every single detail

184
00:05:47,273 --> 00:05:48,453
of your mother's recipe.

185
00:05:48,833 --> 00:05:50,473
You hold their hands when you leave.

186
00:05:50,733 --> 00:05:52,573
And for the first time in your life,

187
00:05:52,813 --> 00:05:54,613
you aren't living in the phantom future.

188
00:05:54,973 --> 00:05:55,633
You are fully,

189
00:05:55,633 --> 00:05:58,213
acutely, painfully alive in the present.

190
00:05:58,912 --> 00:06:00,152
Day seven arrives.

191
00:06:00,492 --> 00:06:01,312
Midnight approaches.

192
00:06:01,892 --> 00:06:04,252
You stand before the mirror in your dark apartment.

193
00:06:04,752 --> 00:06:06,572
Your own forehead is ticking down.

194
00:06:06,572 --> 00:06:08,072
Fifty-two years.

195
00:06:08,292 --> 00:06:09,412
You look at the numbers,

196
00:06:09,612 --> 00:06:10,752
and the fear is gone.

197
00:06:11,012 --> 00:06:13,792
Instead, a profound reverence fills the room.

198
00:06:14,032 --> 00:06:15,432
If human life were infinite,

199
00:06:15,832 --> 00:06:17,632
no decision would ever carry weight.

200
00:06:17,832 --> 00:06:20,312
You could learn guitar in five hundred years.

201
00:06:20,552 --> 00:06:22,532
You could apologize in three centuries.

202
00:06:23,052 --> 00:06:24,812
You could forgive someone tomorrow,

203
00:06:25,072 --> 00:06:26,252
or in a millennium.

204
00:06:26,332 --> 00:06:28,032
Because nothing would ever be lost.

205
00:06:28,032 --> 00:06:30,132
A painting is only beautiful because

206
00:06:30,132 --> 00:06:31,872
of the frame that encloses it.

207
00:06:32,072 --> 00:06:33,952
Music is only heartbreaking because

208
00:06:33,952 --> 00:06:35,712
the chord must eventually end.

209
00:06:35,892 --> 00:06:38,472
And love only burns with divine intensity because

210
00:06:38,472 --> 00:06:40,832
the people we love can be taken from us.

211
00:06:40,992 --> 00:06:42,732
The digital clock reaches eleven

212
00:06:42,732 --> 00:06:44,632
fifty-nine and fifty seconds.

213
00:06:44,632 --> 00:06:45,872
Ten seconds.

214
00:06:45,872 --> 00:06:46,632
Five.

215
00:06:46,632 --> 00:06:47,392
Four.

216
00:06:47,392 --> 00:06:48,152
Three.

217
00:06:48,152 --> 00:06:48,912
Two.

218
00:06:48,912 --> 00:06:49,672
One.

219
00:06:49,672 --> 00:06:52,032
The glowing blue digits dissolve into thin air.

220
00:06:52,412 --> 00:06:54,312
Your reflection looks back at you.

221
00:06:54,612 --> 00:06:55,232
Smooth brow.

222
00:06:55,632 --> 00:06:56,172
No countdown.

223
00:06:56,832 --> 00:06:57,392
No spoilers.

224
00:06:57,392 --> 00:07:00,232
You don't know if you have fifty years left,

225
00:07:00,232 --> 00:07:02,572
fifty days, or fifty minutes.

226
00:07:02,912 --> 00:07:04,212
And that is the greatest gift

227
00:07:04,212 --> 00:07:05,912
the universe ever gave you.

228
00:07:05,972 --> 00:07:08,072
Because the only day that ever matters is

229
00:07:08,072 --> 00:07:10,072
the one you are standing in right now.

230
00:07:10,312 --> 00:07:12,312
Tomorrow, the countdown disappears.

231
00:07:12,872 --> 00:07:14,092
Until you meet someone,

232
00:07:14,552 --> 00:07:15,712
look into their eyes,

233
00:07:15,912 --> 00:07:17,512
and see a word floating above their

234
00:07:17,512 --> 00:07:19,072
lips every time they speak.

235
00:07:19,272 --> 00:07:21,432
What if you could see every single lie?

236
00:07:21,672 --> 00:07:22,192
Subscribe.

237
00:07:22,712 --> 00:07:25,032
Because tomorrow, the truth comes out.
